%[text] 2D and 3D Plots
%[text] 1.Line Plot
%[text] To create two-dimensional line plots , use the plot functions. For example plot the sine functions over a linearly
x = linspace(0,2*pi) %[output:67245050]
y = sin(x) %[output:9ba02202]
plot(x,y) %[output:8e9ee0f7]
%[text] You can label the axes and add a title
xlabel("x") %[output:8e9ee0f7]
ylabel("sin(x)") %[output:8e9ee0f7]
title("Plot of Sine function") %[output:8e9ee0f7]
%[text] By adding a third input argument to the plot function, you can plot the same variable using a red dashed line.
plot(x,y,"r--") %[output:42d19979]
%[text] `"r--"` is a *line specification*. Each specification can include characters for the line color, style, and marker. A marker is a symbol that appears at each plotted data point, such as a `+`, `o`, or `*`. For example, "`g:*"` requests a dotted green line with `*` markers.
%[text] Notice that the titles and labels that you defined for the first plot are no longer in the current figure window. By default, MATLAB® clears the figure each time you call a plotting function, resetting the axes and other elements to prepare the new plot.
%[text] To add plots to an existing figure, use `hold on`. Until you use `hold off` or close the window, all plots appear in the current figure window.
x = linspace(0,2*pi);
y = sin(x);
plot(x,y) %[output:400343f6]

hold on %[output:400343f6]

y2 = cos(x);
plot(x,y2,":") %[output:400343f6]
legend("sin","cos") %[output:400343f6]

hold off %[output:400343f6]
%[text] 2.3D Plots
%[text] Three-dimensional plots typically display a surface defined by a function in two variables, *z*=*f*(*x*,*y*). For instance, calculate *z*=*xe*−*x^*2−*y^*2 given row and column vectors `x` and `y` with 20 points each in the range \[-2,2\].
x = linspace(-2,2,20);
y = x';
z = x .* exp(-x.^2 - y.^2) %[output:12cb10e3]
%[text] Then create a surface plot
surf(x,y,z) %[output:7368e0ed]
%[text] Both the `surf` function and its companion `mesh` display surfaces in three dimensions. `surf` displays both the connecting lines and the faces of the surface in color. `mesh` produces wireframe surfaces that color only the connecting lines.
%[text] 3.Multiple Plots
%[text] You can display multiple plots in different parts of the same window using either `tiledlayout` or `subplot`.
%[text] The `tiledlayout` function was introduced in R2019b and provides more control over labels and spacing than `subplot`. For example, create a 2-by-2 layout within a figure window. Then, call `nexttile` each time you want a plot to appear in the next region.
t = tiledlayout(2,2); %[output:7368e0ed] %[output:8478e7e8]
title(t,"Trignometric Functions") %[output:8478e7e8]
x = linspace(0,30);

nexttile %[output:8478e7e8]
plot(x,sin(x)) %[output:8478e7e8]
title("Sine") %[output:8478e7e8]

nexttile %[output:8478e7e8]
plot(x,cos(x)) %[output:8478e7e8]
title("Cosine") %[output:8478e7e8]

nexttile %[output:8478e7e8]
plot(x,tan(x)) %[output:8478e7e8]
title("Tangent") %[output:8478e7e8]

nexttile %[output:8478e7e8]
plot(x,sec(x)) %[output:8478e7e8]
title("Secant") %[output:8478e7e8]
%[text] 

